ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415857 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415857 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186415788 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415857 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "daa742fd-2059-58a9-8c17-15a7e5c8ca78" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415858 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186415800 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415858 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186415815 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415858 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186415822 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415858 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186415827 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415858 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186415833 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415858 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415858 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415858 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186415827 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415858 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "edcf7bc8-f757-5f28-b389-f3fbae1ad417" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415859 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186415779 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415859 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186415793 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415859 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186415807 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415859 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186415808 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415859 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186415826 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415859 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415859 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415859 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186415779 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415859 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "fbfb1dc9-ac7f-533d-9966-b616b6bc9a03" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415860 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186415847 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415860 #edb/ref 1069 "q-109288" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415860 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415860 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the triangle $C$ in the plane with vertices $(-1,0),(1,0)$ and $(-1,2),$ and the region $D$ bounded by $C.$ Express ${∮}_{C}({y}^{2}-2xy)\\,dx+(x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y-{x}^{2})\\,dy$ as a repeated integral." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415860 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "0fd75856-9255-5679-88e6-6aeda24c9a6c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415860 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D$, then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/ea/eac7a442f0a794db0606b95aa722320d099f2bcc4e530fc6b62e9be889335da2.png)\n\n \n\nWe have that $P(x,y)={y}^{2}-2xy$ and $Q(x,y)=x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y-{x}^{2}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=2y-2x,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡y-2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type I region as follows:\n\n$$\nD={(x,y):-1\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 1-x}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's Theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}({y}^{2}-2xy)\\,dx+(x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y-{x}^{2})\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}[\\operatorname{sin}⁡y-2x-(2y-2x)]\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡y-2y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1-x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡y-2y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415861 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186415848 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415861 #edb/ref 1069 "q-109248" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415861 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415861 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the closed curve $C,$ which is the boundary of the region enclosed by the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1$ and the $y$-axis, for $x\\ge 0.$ Express ${∮}_{C}(x{y}^{2}-{e}^{x})\\,dx+(2{x}^{2}y-1)\\,dy$ as a repeated integral." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415861 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "196f72ff-1ff2-5e8e-8905-6141cb15ad66" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415861 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D$, then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/92/92b9a6c83dd18a5599bbee71d714b26fddd62eb32917a5df983edc232664067b.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=x{y}^{2}-{e}^{x}$ and $Q(x,y)=2{x}^{2}y-1.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=2xy,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=4xy.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type II region as follows:\n\n$$\nD={(x,y):0\\le x\\le \\sqrt{1-{y}^{2}},\\,-1\\le y\\le 1}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's Theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}(x{y}^{2}-{e}^{x})\\,dx+(2{x}^{2}y-1)\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(4xy-2xy)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2xy\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{1-{y}^{2}}}2xy\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415862 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186415850 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415862 #edb/ref 1069 "q-109196" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415862 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415862 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the rectangle $C$ in the plane with vertices $(0,1),(0,-1),(1,-1)$ and $(1,1),$ and the region $D$ bounded by $C.$ According to Green's theorem, \n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})\\,dx-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)\\,dy={∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nCalculate the function $f(x,y).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415862 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "25b5bd80-ef51-5481-b2fb-7d3f90359e46" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415862 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D$, then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/a3/a3e81cfacc47dfa241a5fea64de84e375a58a6d86ceab82c86303763d5e41fe5.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)={x}^{2}-{y}^{2}$ and $Q(x,y)=-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy).$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-2y,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=-2y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})\\,dx-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}[-2y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)-(-2y)]\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-2y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)+2y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2y(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy))\\,\\text{d}A\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f(x,y)=2y(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415863 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186415852 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415863 #edb/ref 1069 "q-109157" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415863 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415863 #edb/ref 1071 "Use Green's theorem to evaluate ${∮}_{C}(2x+{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y)\\,dx+(x+{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y)\\,dy,$ where $C$ is the boundary of the trapezoid with vertices at $(0,1),$ $(0,-5),$ $(4,-1)$ and $(4,1).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415863 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "4383616d-c261-5116-9385-9c929fedb95e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415863 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D$, then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/ac/acd7ae0cc90007b9f710f242ab1f0498b2439cdbb8951df388b9d8d0588d553f.png)\n\nFirst, notice that we can rewrite our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∮}_{C}(2x+{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y)\\,dx+(x+{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y)\\,dy\n$$\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=2x+{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y$ and $Q(x,y)=x+{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}={e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x} & =1+{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type I region as follows:\n\n$$\nD={(x,y):0\\le x\\le 4,\\,x-5\\le y\\le 1}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's Theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}(2x+{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y)\\,dx+(x+{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y)\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(1+{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y-{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}1\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}{\\int }_{x-5}^{1}\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}[y]_{x-5}^{1}\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}(6-x)\\,dx \\\\  & ={[6x-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}]}_{0}^{4} \\\\  & =24-\\frac{16}{2} \\\\  & =16.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415864 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186415858 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415864 #edb/ref 1069 "q-109199" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415864 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415864 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the triangle $C$ in the plane with vertices $(-1,0),(0,-1)$ and $(1,1),$ and the region $D$ bounded by $C.$ According to Green's theorem, \n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2xy)\\,dx+2{sin}^{2}⁡(xy)\\,dy={∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nCalculate the function $f(x,y).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415864 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "4af8cfd2-bdac-5f91-b83d-a441b5fa79ce" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415864 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D$, then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/f4/f4bb6636c104cf17bcd4b25037a3490ba30b7c1380b152c33504d9015903bdca.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2xy)$ and $Q(x,y)=2{sin}^{2}⁡xy.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2xy),\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=4y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)=2y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2xy).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2xy)\\,dx+2{sin}^{2}⁡(xy)\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(2y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2xy)-(-2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2xy)))\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(2y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2xy)+2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2xy))\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2(x+y)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2xy)\\,\\text{d}A\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f(x,y)=2(x+y)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2xy).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415865 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186415859 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415865 #edb/ref 1069 "q-109129" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415865 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415865 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the path $C,$ in the plane, as the boundary of the region enclosed by the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1$ and the $x$-axis, for $y\\ge 0,$ and the region $D$ bounded by $C.$ According to Green's theorem, \n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}x{e}^{-{y}^{2}}\\,dx+{x}^{2}y{e}^{-{y}^{2}}\\,dy={∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nCalculate the function $f(x,y).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415865 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "581c6229-8cb3-5361-87bc-faf26b91b942" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415865 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D$, then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/a0/a0d50e8f2faf679e6dee1471c8fa1ca2be4194796e606fc3e89372f0fefe8cfc.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=x{e}^{-{y}^{2}}$ and $Q(x,y)={x}^{2}y{e}^{-{y}^{2}}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-2xy{e}^{-{y}^{2}},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=2xy{e}^{-{y}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}x{e}^{-{y}^{2}}\\,dx+{x}^{2}y{e}^{-{y}^{2}}\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}[2xy{e}^{-{y}^{2}}-(-2xy{e}^{-{y}^{2}})]\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(2xy{e}^{-{y}^{2}}+2xy{e}^{-{y}^{2}})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}4xy{e}^{-{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f(x,y)=4xy{e}^{-{y}^{2}}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415866 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186415856 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415866 #edb/ref 1069 "q-41475" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415866 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415866 #edb/ref 1071 "Use Green's theorem to evaluate ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr,$ where $F(x,y)=⟨6{x}^{2}-xy+11,{y}^{2}+1⟩$ and $C$ is the boundary of the triangle with vertices at $(0,0),$ $(2,-1)$ and $(2,1).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415866 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "5bd170e0-be10-5a08-9034-fb1371f7f685" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415866 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D$, then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/00/007634ca34871f05cde9d44a58fcf24b1e5fd3b8c337c68f2025fb8724eef819.png)\n\nFirst, notice that we can rewrite our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∮}_{C}(6{x}^{2}-xy+11)\\,dx+({y}^{2}+1)\\,dy\n$$\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=6{x}^{2}-xy+11$ and $Q(x,y)={y}^{2}+1.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-x,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type I region as follows:\n\n$$\nD={(x,y):0\\le x\\le 2,-\\frac{x}{2}\\le y\\le \\frac{x}{2}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's Theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}(6{x}^{2}-xy+11)\\,dx+({y}^{2}+1)\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}[0-(-x)]\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}x\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{-x/2}^{x/2}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}x[y]_{-x/2}^{x/2}\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}x(x)\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{x}^{2}\\,dx \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415867 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186415857 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415867 #edb/ref 1069 "q-109120" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415867 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415867 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the path $C,$ in the plane, as the boundary of the region enclosed by the circle ${x}^{2}+(y-1)^{2}=1$ and the $y$-axis, for $x\\ge 0,$ and the region $D$ bounded by $C.$ According to Green's theorem, \n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}2{y}^{3/2}\\,dx+(x+2x\\sqrt{y})\\,dy={∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nCalculate the function $f(x,y).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415867 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "631300c6-4369-57db-a4e4-4ed4d7169680" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415867 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D$, then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/93/93cb529eecb888a110eaad8f55ec458b3710f5a37b497aeed38fa32621178625.png)\n\n \n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=2{y}^{3/2}$ and $Q(x,y)=x+2x\\sqrt{y}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=3\\sqrt{y},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=1+2\\sqrt{y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}2{y}^{3/2}\\,dx+(x+2x\\sqrt{y})\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(1+2\\sqrt{y}-3\\sqrt{y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(1-\\sqrt{y})\\,\\text{d}A\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f(x,y)=1-\\sqrt{y}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415868 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186415855 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415868 #edb/ref 1069 "q-109149" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415868 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415868 #edb/ref 1071 "Use Green's theorem to evaluate ${∮}_{C}xy\\,dx+(2{x}^{2}+y)\\,dy,$ where $C$ is the boundary of the rectangle with vertices at $(-1,0),$ $(3,0),$ $(3,2)$ and $(-1,2).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415868 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "6fbdc55e-ae46-5858-9840-dcfa0a36807f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415868 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D$, then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/39/3954846460076fa1ef1e5826d32bde9145e7350cb2324072b001d8c6ff41cc14.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=xy$ and $Q(x,y)=2{x}^{2}+y.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=x,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x} & =4x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type I region as follows:\n\n$$\nD={(x,y):-1\\le x\\le 3,\\,0\\le y\\le 2}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's Theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}xy\\,dx+(2{x}^{2}+y)\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(4x-x)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}3x\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{2}3x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{3}3x[y]_{0}^{2}\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{3}3x(2)\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{3}6x\\,dx \\\\  & =3[{x}^{2}]_{-1}^{3} \\\\  & =3[9-1] \\\\  & =24.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415869 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186415849 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415869 #edb/ref 1069 "q-109144" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415869 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415869 #edb/ref 1071 "Use Green's theorem to evaluate ${∮}_{C}(2xy-{y}^{2})\\,dx+({x}^{2}+2x)\\,dy,$ where $C$ is the boundary of the triangle with vertices at $(-1,0),$ $(1,0)$ and $(-1,2).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415869 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "8c2f5a35-b3df-565f-bebb-8a3475dfd0d6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415869 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D$, then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/cb/cb85e51584f0c41fc19210a5ce56289baa652aff3046d5d88c034d187b7e45dd.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=2xy-{y}^{2}$ and $Q(x,y)={x}^{2}+2x.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=2x-2y,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x} & =2x+2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type I region as follows:\n\n$$\nD={(x,y):-1\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 1-x}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's Theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}(2xy-{y}^{2})\\,dx+({x}^{2}+2x)\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(2x+2-2x+2y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(2+2y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1-x}(2+2y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{[2y+{y}^{2}]}_{0}^{1-x}\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}(2(1-x)+(1-x)^{2})dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}({x}^{2}-4x+3)\\,dx \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}-2{x}^{2}+3x]}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =[\\frac{1}{3}-2+3]-[-\\frac{1}{3}-2-3] \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}+6 \\\\  & =\\frac{20}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415870 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186415846 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415870 #edb/ref 1069 "q-109245" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415870 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415870 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the rectangle $C$ in the plane with vertices $(-2,0),(1,0),(1,1)$ and $(-2,1),$ and the region $D$ bounded by $C.$ Express ${∮}_{C}y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,dx+(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+x{y}^{2})\\,dy$ as a repeated integral." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415870 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "c14fef09-0118-528c-b9c9-91bf74ebf7eb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415870 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D$, then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/a5/a5404053d83188af931c19350d5b6e42145009cf7f67b9dffcc08edc16ad3627.png)\n\n \n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ and $Q(x,y)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+x{y}^{2}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+{y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type I region as follows:\n\n$$\nD={(x,y):-2\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 1}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's Theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,dx+(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+x{y}^{2})\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+{y}^{2}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415871 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186415854 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415871 #edb/ref 1069 "q-108830" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415871 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415871 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the triangle $C$ in the plane with vertices $(0,0),(2,0)$ and $(0,2),$ and the region $D$ bounded by $C.$ According to Green's theorem, \n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}{x}^{2}{y}^{2}\\,dx+({x}^{3}y+\\operatorname{sin}⁡y)\\,dy={∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nCalculate the function $f(x,y).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415871 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "dfcce989-6529-57a3-8788-c6f5ffffe36a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415871 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D$, then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/2b/2bec8a0243d2ea9e983cab4c07719ff4a5d309960039ccdd77e621fd15ffd7ed.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)={x}^{2}{y}^{2}$ and $Q(x,y)={x}^{3}y+y.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=2{x}^{2}y,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=3{x}^{2}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}{x}^{2}{y}^{2}\\,dx+({x}^{3}y+\\operatorname{sin}⁡y)\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(3{x}^{2}y-2{x}^{2}y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}{x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}A\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f(x,y)={x}^{2}y.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
